note: library function names and words with their own expression syntax are not reserved: they can be
note: variable, function, parameter and loop-variable names, declared and read back. (The reserved words
note: of the statements -- add, to, in, not, global, ... -- cannot be names: tests/errors/syntax.)

number count = 10
number by = 2
count = count + 1
print(count)
print(by)

note: the same name can also be reused as a function (variables and functions have separate namespaces)
func total(number x, number y) do:
    return x + y
end
number total = 5
print(total)

note: also usable as a loop variable name
loop repeat number step = 1 ~ 3 do:
    count = count + step
end
print(count)

note: match/find/replace/split/count have their own expression syntax,
note: but are treated as plain identifiers when that syntax does not follow.
str replace = "R"
str split = "S"
str find = "F"
str match = "M"
print(replace)
print(split)
print(find)
print(match)

note: and the real syntax must still work.
use DLC:string
print(split("a,b,c", p","))
print(find("hello world", p"world"))
print(replace("hello world", p"world", "there"))
print(count("banana", p"a"))

note: the "is not" comparison is a word of its own.
boolean flag = true
if flag is not false do:
    print("is-not still works")
end
